00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:13,800
So, I want to say something.

154
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:19,800
I don't know if I gave enough emphasis to the central limit

155
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:24,800
theorem yesterday, last lecture.

156
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:31,800
This is a fundamental theorem from probability theory, which

157
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:40,800
says that if you have independent identity, you can

158
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:55,800
distribute identically distributed random variables.

159
00:08:55,800 --> 00:09:00,800
That is, every random variable is independent of the other,

160
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:03,800
and they all have the same distribution.

161
00:09:03,800 --> 00:09:08,800
And if it has a finite variance, then the distribution of an

162
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:10,800
average distribution of a random variable, which is a finite

163
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:17,800
average of these variables, converges to the normal distribution

164
00:09:17,800 --> 00:09:22,800
as the number of elements in the average is increased.

165
00:09:22,800 --> 00:09:26,800
In other words, averages are approximately normally distributed.

166
00:09:26,800 --> 00:09:32,800
The bell-shaped curve works as well as it does, because so many

167
00:09:32,800 --> 00:09:35,800
things we observe in nature are averages.

168
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,800
So many things that we observe are the sum of many effects.

169
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:40,800
This is what history is all about.

170
00:09:40,800 --> 00:09:44,800
Anytime big events occur, it's probably because a number of

171
00:09:44,800 --> 00:09:47,800
things chance to happen at the same time.

172
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:52,800
And that's why probability theorists think that we know,

173
00:09:52,800 --> 00:09:56,800
we kind of know the probability distribution of, and that's this

174
00:09:56,800 --> 00:10:02,800
bell-shaped curve, which I had on a slide last period.

175
00:10:02,800 --> 00:10:05,800
The critical thing about the bell-shaped curve is that the

176
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:09,800
tails drop off really fast after you get a certain distance out.

177
00:10:09,800 --> 00:10:11,800
And either way, they essentially hit zero.

178
00:10:11,800 --> 00:10:13,800
They never hit zero.

179
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:19,800
The probability is never zero that anything can happen with a

180
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:22,800
normally distributed variable.

181
00:10:22,800 --> 00:10:29,800
But the normal distribution does not have fat tails.

182
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:33,800
After two or three or four standard deviations, basically

183
00:10:33,800 --> 00:10:37,800
the probability is zero that that kind of thing will happen,

184
00:10:37,800 --> 00:10:39,800
that it returns.

185
00:10:39,800 --> 00:10:43,800
And we can't even predict the return if we're applying it to

186
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:47,800
returns, that that would occur.

187
00:10:47,800 --> 00:10:51,800
So, the problem with the central limit theorem is that

188
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:53,800
it's only as good as its assumptions.

189
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:57,800
And it assumes that the underlying variables have a

190
00:10:57,800 --> 00:10:59,800
finite variance.

191
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:04,800
That they themselves are not so fat-tailed that the variance of

192
00:11:04,800 --> 00:11:06,800
them is infinite.

193
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:09,800
And in fact, that assumption might be wrong.

194
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:14,800
Because we see, especially in finance, we see big outliers

195
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:19,800
coming from time to time.

196
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:22,800
And so, I have to present a theory and say, I like the

197
00:11:22,800 --> 00:11:24,800
theory, the central limit theorem is an important theory,

198
00:11:24,800 --> 00:11:26,800
but it's also wrong.

199
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:29,800
That's the problem with financial theory.

200
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:33,800
It's not quite as good as the theory that physicists or

201
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:35,800
chemists use.

202
00:11:35,800 --> 00:11:37,800
It has its limitations.

203
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:40,800
Nonetheless, that's what we have to talk about.

204
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:46,800
And we have to make do with that as much as we can.

