263
00:15:38,800 --> 00:15:40,800
What else did they not have?

264
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:46,800
They didn't have electronic trading.

265
00:15:46,800 --> 00:15:49,800
They would do trading by word of mouth.

266
00:15:49,800 --> 00:15:53,800
They would meet together on the floor of an exchange and shout

267
00:15:53,800 --> 00:15:55,800
at each other and talk.

268
00:15:55,800 --> 00:15:59,800
I guess they had telephones, but it was all words and people.

269
00:15:59,800 --> 00:16:02,800
And writing on paper.

270
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:10,800
So, that's the proliferation of financial instruments in 40 years

271
00:16:10,800 --> 00:16:13,800
is stunning.

272
00:16:13,800 --> 00:16:17,800
And so, I wanted to use that as a springboard to think about,

273
00:16:17,800 --> 00:16:20,800
what is the next 40 years going to be like?

274
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:24,800
And if any of you go into finance, this will be your career.

275
00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:28,800
I think that it's reasonable to suppose that the transformation in the

276
00:16:28,800 --> 00:16:33,800
next 40 years is going to be just as dramatic as the last 40

277
00:16:33,800 --> 00:16:35,800
years, or more so.

278
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:39,800
Because technical progress doesn't slow down.

279
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:45,800
I don't see any reason to think that it's slowing down.

280
00:16:45,800 --> 00:16:48,800
So, I like to think about the future, but it's hard to talk

281
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:51,800
about the future, because we're not there yet.

282
00:16:51,800 --> 00:16:53,800
I've written a couple of books.

283
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:56,800
One of them is really about the future of finance.

284
00:16:56,800 --> 00:16:58,800
And I think it's really important to think about the future of

285
00:16:58,800 --> 00:16:59,800
finance.

286
00:16:59,800 --> 00:17:05,800
One of them is called Macro Markets, which I wrote in 1993,

287
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:06,800
about the big market.

288
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:09,800
Macro means big markets that we'll be seeing in the future.

289
00:17:09,800 --> 00:17:12,800
And the other one was called New Financial Order, which I wrote

290
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:14,800
in 2003.

291
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:16,800
And we have the introductory chapter there on the reading

292
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:21,800
list for this lecture.

293
00:17:21,800 --> 00:17:28,800
But the basic, what I see is happening with all of these,

294
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:31,800
the big markets, and the big markets, and the big markets

295
00:17:31,800 --> 00:17:34,800
are the ones that are going to be the big markets.

296
00:17:34,800 --> 00:17:38,800
Incidentally, we have a lot of questioning of these inventions

297
00:17:38,800 --> 00:17:41,800
now, because they kind of blew up on us in this financial

298
00:17:41,800 --> 00:17:42,800
crisis.

299
00:17:42,800 --> 00:17:46,800
So, it's a little bit like after an airplane crash, people

300
00:17:46,800 --> 00:17:49,800
are kind of critical of the aeronautical engineers for a

301
00:17:49,800 --> 00:17:50,800
while.

302
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:54,800
Or when they invented steam engines, some of them blew up.

303
00:17:54,800 --> 00:17:55,800
And it was bad.

304
00:17:55,800 --> 00:17:57,800
You know, when a boiler blows up, it scalds all the people

305
00:17:57,800 --> 00:17:59,800
who were mad at them for a while.

306
00:17:59,800 --> 00:18:01,800
I think that's what we're going through now.

307
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:05,800
And people are angry about some of these inventions.

308
00:18:05,800 --> 00:18:11,800
But that's just part of progress.

309
00:18:11,800 --> 00:18:14,800
Well, it doesn't mean that we don't want to regulate them.

310
00:18:14,800 --> 00:18:18,800
We regulate boilers and airplanes right now to prevent

311
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:22,800
crashes, and we need to regulate our financial markets

312
00:18:22,800 --> 00:18:23,800
just as well.

313
00:18:23,800 --> 00:18:25,800
But, yeah.

314
00:18:25,800 --> 00:18:26,800
Thank you.

315
00:18:26,800 --> 00:18:27,800
Thank you.

316
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:43,800
So, the financial markets that we have, they're based on

317
00:18:43,800 --> 00:18:44,800
mathematical models.

318
00:18:44,800 --> 00:18:47,800
This is a mathematical discipline, but the

319
00:18:47,800 --> 00:18:52,800
mathematical models depend only on certain intuition.

320
00:18:52,800 --> 00:18:56,800
Maybe a core part of the intuition that underlies the

321
00:18:56,800 --> 00:19:04,800
financial inventions is the idea of independence.

322
00:19:04,800 --> 00:19:07,800
And that if risks are, I'm going to try and talk in

323
00:19:07,800 --> 00:19:08,800
really basic terms.

324
00:19:08,800 --> 00:19:11,800
If risks are independent of each other, then we can pull

325
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:14,800
them and they go away.

326
00:19:14,800 --> 00:19:18,800
That's the core idea.

327
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:22,800
But how to make that happen requires some thought and some

328
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:24,800
devices.

329
00:19:24,800 --> 00:19:30,800
The intuitive idea that we can exploit independence to improve

330
00:19:30,800 --> 00:19:34,800
our lives goes back to ancient times, actually.

331
00:19:34,800 --> 00:19:39,800
A lot of things that people do are done in recognition of what

332
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:42,800
is independent and what is not.

333
00:19:42,800 --> 00:19:46,800
I think even in big causes that people get emotional about over

334
00:19:46,800 --> 00:19:52,800
the ages are causes that, I can think of them as examples of the

335
00:19:52,800 --> 00:19:55,800
probability theory in modern finance.

336
00:19:55,800 --> 00:19:59,800
So, you might not agree with me, but this is my view of it.

337
00:19:59,800 --> 00:20:01,800
Think of socialism.

338
00:20:01,800 --> 00:20:03,800
What is socialism?

339
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:06,800
Well, you know it was invented by the philosopher or

340
00:20:06,800 --> 00:20:08,800
entrepreneur Robert Owen.

341
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:12,800
The word was invented by him in the early 19th century.

342
00:20:12,800 --> 00:20:13,800
But what is it?

343
00:20:13,800 --> 00:20:17,800
It is that society gets together and pools all of its activities.

344
00:20:17,800 --> 00:20:19,800
And I think, well, why would you want to do that?

345
00:20:19,800 --> 00:20:22,800
Well, one reason you do that is that it improves

346
00:20:22,800 --> 00:20:25,800
human welfare, because it shares risks.

347
00:20:25,800 --> 00:20:29,800
And we're all in it together, and we won't have rich and poor.

348
00:20:29,800 --> 00:20:32,800
I think that's part of Robert Owen's idea.

349
00:20:32,800 --> 00:20:36,800
He was worried about inequality, that some people are much better

350
00:20:36,800 --> 00:20:38,800
off than others, and that's bad.

351
00:20:38,800 --> 00:20:41,800
And so, let's create a socialist society.

352
00:20:41,800 --> 00:20:46,800
So, he had an invention of sorts, but it didn't seem to be a very

353
00:20:46,800 --> 00:20:50,800
– in his form, it was not a very successful invention, because

354
00:20:50,800 --> 00:20:51,800
there was –

355
00:20:51,800 --> 00:20:53,800
it didn't work somehow.

356
00:20:53,800 --> 00:20:57,800
He set up a town called New Harmony in the United States,

357
00:20:57,800 --> 00:21:00,800
which was supposed to be harmonious by the name of the town,

358
00:21:00,800 --> 00:21:02,800
and everything was shared.

359
00:21:02,800 --> 00:21:05,800
And unfortunately, they ended up arguing and fighting

360
00:21:05,800 --> 00:21:07,800
amongst each other, and they were not happy.

361
00:21:07,800 --> 00:21:09,800
He didn't get it figured out right.

362
00:21:09,800 --> 00:21:12,800
He was trying to pool risks, but he didn't do it.

363
00:21:12,800 --> 00:21:16,800
There are other idealistic societies that try to do it,

364
00:21:16,800 --> 00:21:19,800
like the kibbutzim in Israel, but that's one of many,

365
00:21:19,800 --> 00:21:21,800
where people get together and do it.

366
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:24,800
They get together and form a community, and they pool

367
00:21:24,800 --> 00:21:27,800
everything, and it kind of works for some people.

368
00:21:27,800 --> 00:21:31,800
But only a tiny fraction of the population in Israel lives on a

369
00:21:31,800 --> 00:21:33,800
kibbutz now.

370
00:21:33,800 --> 00:21:34,800
Why not?

371
00:21:34,800 --> 00:21:37,800
I think most people just don't fit in that way.

372
00:21:37,800 --> 00:21:40,800
We share everything.

373
00:21:40,800 --> 00:21:43,800
After a few months living there, you might think, I'm out of here.

374
00:21:43,800 --> 00:21:48,800
I don't want to share everything with everybody.

375
00:21:48,800 --> 00:21:49,800
So, people do things.

376
00:21:49,800 --> 00:21:55,800
And in the old west, the pioneers in America, they had

377
00:21:55,800 --> 00:21:59,800
kind of a social compact that if one farmer's house burned

378
00:21:59,800 --> 00:22:02,800
down, everyone will come by and help and erect a new house for

379
00:22:02,800 --> 00:22:03,800
that person.

380
00:22:03,800 --> 00:22:06,800
So, that's, again, risk management.

381
00:22:06,800 --> 00:22:07,800
And it works.

382
00:22:07,800 --> 00:22:11,800
It sounds almost like a moral thing, but it works only

383
00:22:11,800 --> 00:22:13,800
because they don't all burn down at the same time.

384
00:22:13,800 --> 00:22:17,800
It would be totally worthless if they all did.

385
00:22:17,800 --> 00:22:20,800
And so, there are primitive ideas of insurance that underlie

386
00:22:20,800 --> 00:22:23,800
that, but the ideas aren't worked out well.

387
00:22:23,800 --> 00:22:26,800
And it doesn't, perhaps, work well to do it on such an

388
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:27,800
informal basis.

389
00:22:27,800 --> 00:22:33,800
So, our society has particular inventions that, we call them

390
00:22:33,800 --> 00:22:35,800
financial or insurance inventions, that make these

391
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:37,800
things work better.

392
00:22:37,800 --> 00:22:46,800
And so, what we're talking about is motivated by theory.

393
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:48,800
And the mathematical theory of finance.

394
00:22:48,800 --> 00:22:50,800
But that's not the subject of this course.

395
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:53,800
I want to, in this course, talk more about the inventions

396
00:22:53,800 --> 00:22:56,800
themselves.

397
00:22:56,800 --> 00:23:04,800
And in particular, we'll be talking about risk management.

398
00:23:04,800 --> 00:23:08,800
Now, this brings up a basic issue about finance.

399
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:10,800
Is finance good?

400
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:14,800
Is it helpful to people?

401
00:23:14,800 --> 00:23:16,800
A huge issue.

402
00:23:16,800 --> 00:23:22,800
A huge issue in the history of humankind is inequality.

403
00:23:22,800 --> 00:23:26,800
And that people get upset when it seems like other people

404
00:23:26,800 --> 00:23:29,800
have an unfair advantage over us.

405
00:23:29,800 --> 00:23:32,800
But inequality is something that you'd think finance

406
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:33,800
works against.

407
00:23:33,800 --> 00:23:36,800
Modern finance is about risk management.

408
00:23:36,800 --> 00:23:39,800
So, you can get rid of the purely random element in

409
00:23:39,800 --> 00:23:40,800
people's lives.

410
00:23:40,800 --> 00:23:43,800
That should make people more equal.

411
00:23:43,800 --> 00:23:44,800
And it should be a good thing.

412
00:23:44,800 --> 00:23:46,800
That is the way I view it.

413
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:49,800
And the other side of finance is that it also creates

414
00:23:49,800 --> 00:23:50,800
opportunity.

415
00:23:50,800 --> 00:23:53,800
And opportunity is very important also.

416
00:23:53,800 --> 00:23:56,800
We can all be equal in living in poverty, and we don't

417
00:23:56,800 --> 00:23:58,800
particularly like that.

418
00:23:58,800 --> 00:24:05,800
So, that's why financial inventions eventually inspire

419
00:24:05,800 --> 00:24:07,800
and get people excited.

420
00:24:07,800 --> 00:24:10,800
I always remember Deng Xiaoping's famous statement in

421
00:24:10,800 --> 00:24:16,800
the late 1970s, when China was adopting modern financial

422
00:24:16,800 --> 00:24:19,800
methods, and some people were getting rich.

423
00:24:19,800 --> 00:24:24,800
And someone asked Deng, isn't this inconsistent with our

424
00:24:24,800 --> 00:24:26,800
ideology?

425
00:24:26,800 --> 00:24:29,800
And then he said, well, I'm quoting him approximately,

426
00:24:29,800 --> 00:24:32,800
we're all going to get rich, but somebody has to get rich

427
00:24:32,800 --> 00:24:33,800
first.

428
00:24:33,800 --> 00:24:35,800
And that's the way it is.

429
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:40,800
Financial markets, they do manage risk, but they also

430
00:24:40,800 --> 00:24:43,800
create opportunities, and that can actually increase

431
00:24:43,800 --> 00:24:45,800
inequality.

432
00:24:45,800 --> 00:24:48,800
So, you have to consider both sides of this.

433
00:24:48,800 --> 00:24:57,800
So, maybe the most important concept in finance really is

434
00:24:57,800 --> 00:24:59,800
risk.

435
00:24:59,800 --> 00:25:05,800
And risk is all about limiting inequality, or at least the

436
00:25:05,800 --> 00:25:09,800
random, gratuitous inequality, right?

437
00:25:09,800 --> 00:25:14,800
People are troubled by, you know, the idea that you can

438
00:25:14,800 --> 00:25:21,800
get rich, but you're troubled by inequality if it's arbitrary.

439
00:25:21,800 --> 00:25:24,800
But, you know, most people won't begrudge someone who

440
00:25:24,800 --> 00:25:29,800
works very hard and shows real genius and insight and makes a

441
00:25:29,800 --> 00:25:30,800
lot of money.

442
00:25:30,800 --> 00:25:33,800
Most people say that's all right.

443
00:25:33,800 --> 00:25:37,800
But we have trouble dealing, now, part of the way we deal

444
00:25:37,800 --> 00:25:42,800
with risk is our taxes, tax and welfare system.

445
00:25:42,800 --> 00:25:45,800
So, our tax and welfare, I'm going to come back to that

446
00:25:45,800 --> 00:25:48,800
mostly in our last, second to the last lecture.

447
00:25:48,800 --> 00:25:53,800
But we have a progressive income tax that taxes rich

448
00:25:53,800 --> 00:25:56,800
people more, and we have welfare.

449
00:25:56,800 --> 00:25:59,800
And we also, I should add, it's not counted as welfare, but

450
00:25:59,800 --> 00:26:04,800
free public education, which is a form of, I wouldn't call it

451
00:26:04,800 --> 00:26:07,800
welfare, but it's equalizing expenditure.

452
00:26:07,800 --> 00:26:10,800
These are actually our most important risk management

453
00:26:10,800 --> 00:26:11,800
devices.

454
00:26:11,800 --> 00:26:15,800
That's what makes life tolerable in modern society,

455
00:26:15,800 --> 00:26:21,800
that we do have progressive taxes and welfare.

456
00:26:21,800 --> 00:26:24,800
But we don't rely on those exclusively.

457
00:26:24,800 --> 00:26:28,800
And so, finance is getting into the other dimensions.

458
00:26:28,800 --> 00:26:32,800
And we kind of want to let insurance, private insurance,

459
00:26:32,800 --> 00:26:35,800
rather than social insurance flourish.

460
00:26:35,800 --> 00:26:38,800
Because when the government handles everything, it doesn't

461
00:26:38,800 --> 00:26:39,800
matter what the government does.

462
00:26:39,800 --> 00:26:40,800
It doesn't matter what the government does.

463
00:26:40,800 --> 00:26:42,800
It doesn't matter what the government does.

464
00:26:42,800 --> 00:26:44,800
It doesn't matter what the government does.

465
00:26:44,800 --> 00:26:47,800
It doesn't seem to work as effectively or creatively as it

466
00:26:47,800 --> 00:26:55,800
can if we let private entrepreneurs handle things.

467
00:26:55,800 --> 00:27:01,800
So, what we're talking about here, to always put it in

468
00:27:01,800 --> 00:27:05,800
perspective, what we're talking about in this course is about

469
00:27:05,800 --> 00:27:10,800
something that is an add-on to an existing welfare and taxes

470
00:27:10,800 --> 00:27:17,800
system, but an add-on that is, we hope, particularly effective.

471
00:27:17,800 --> 00:27:22,800
And it's added on by people in their own self-interest, or

472
00:27:22,800 --> 00:27:28,800
maybe for their own purposes, but something that ultimately

473
00:27:28,800 --> 00:27:32,800
contributes to, I think, a better world.

474
00:27:32,800 --> 00:27:38,800
And that's what I want to.

475
00:27:38,800 --> 00:27:40,800
So, I have several themes in this.

476
00:27:40,800 --> 00:27:45,800
The first is a risk theme, which I've just discussed.

477
00:27:45,800 --> 00:27:53,800
Another theme I want to talk about here is a framing theme.

478
00:27:53,800 --> 00:28:00,800
And that is that psychologists use the term framing to refer

479
00:28:00,800 --> 00:28:08,800
to the context and associations that we have with something.

480
00:28:08,800 --> 00:28:15,800
So, the way people use things depends on what they see them

481
00:28:15,800 --> 00:28:22,800
associated with, because people can't think through to the

482
00:28:22,800 --> 00:28:25,800
fundamental theory all the time.

483
00:28:25,800 --> 00:28:29,800
So, we have to frame things in a way that's convenient to people.

484
00:28:29,800 --> 00:28:31,800
And this is part of financial engineering.

485
00:28:31,800 --> 00:28:35,800
I'll come back to that in a minute.

486
00:28:35,800 --> 00:28:37,800
But then.

487
00:28:37,800 --> 00:28:42,800
But framing has to do with language and names that we give to things.

488
00:28:42,800 --> 00:28:50,800
We have to design inventions so that they are framed in good ways.

489
00:28:50,800 --> 00:28:52,800
We'll explain that in a minute.

490
00:28:52,800 --> 00:28:54,800
And then I have a device theme.

491
00:28:54,800 --> 00:29:01,800
That finance is really about devices, like steam engines.

492
00:29:01,800 --> 00:29:05,800
We call them financial contracts.

493
00:29:05,800 --> 00:29:18,800
And devices are complicated structures that we set up for a certain purpose.

494
00:29:18,800 --> 00:29:22,800
And like airplanes or automobiles.

495
00:29:22,800 --> 00:29:29,800
And we learn through time how to make them better and better.

496
00:29:29,800 --> 00:29:33,800
And they tend to get.

497
00:29:33,800 --> 00:29:37,800
They tend to come on the world with a flourish, a surprise.

498
00:29:37,800 --> 00:29:40,800
There's some new invention.

499
00:29:40,800 --> 00:29:47,800
You know, when the Wright brothers first exhibited the airplane in the Paris Air Show in 1904,

500
00:29:47,800 --> 00:29:49,800
I think, and people were stunned.

501
00:29:49,800 --> 00:29:51,800
They were actually flying.

502
00:29:51,800 --> 00:29:59,800
And it immediately set up around the world, the aircraft industry.

503
00:29:59,800 --> 00:30:02,800
If you look around the world, these devices look very similar.

504
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:07,800
Automobiles, airplanes, they look almost the same in every country.

505
00:30:07,800 --> 00:30:14,800
But that's because they have an internal logic to them that makes them work well.

506
00:30:14,800 --> 00:30:19,800
And the logic may not be apparent to you.

507
00:30:19,800 --> 00:30:25,800
You might not, probably generally don't fully understand why a device works as well as it does.

508
00:30:25,800 --> 00:30:28,800
Well, I have an example of a simple invention.

509
00:30:28,800 --> 00:30:30,800
And I was curious.

510
00:30:30,800 --> 00:30:32,800
I haven't used this example before.

511
00:30:32,800 --> 00:30:36,800
I'm going to show you how many of you use this invention.

512
00:30:36,800 --> 00:30:40,800
Do you know what a gimlet is?

513
00:30:40,800 --> 00:30:44,800
How many of you own a gimlet?

514
00:30:44,800 --> 00:30:45,800
Nobody.

515
00:30:45,800 --> 00:30:47,800
Nobody owns a gimlet.

516
00:30:47,800 --> 00:30:52,800
Can someone tell me what a gimlet is?

517
00:30:52,800 --> 00:30:54,800
It's amazing.

518
00:30:54,800 --> 00:30:58,800
I shouldn't be.

519
00:30:58,800 --> 00:31:00,800
A gimlet is a simple tool

520
00:31:00,800 --> 00:31:04,800
that everyone used to have 100 years ago.

521
00:31:04,800 --> 00:31:09,800
And what does it look like?

522
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:18,800
It's made out of wire.

523
00:31:26,800 --> 00:31:28,800
This is a handle.

524
00:31:28,800 --> 00:31:29,800
Okay?

525
00:31:29,800 --> 00:31:31,800
Do you have a gimlet now?

526
00:31:31,800 --> 00:31:33,800
Now that I've drawn a picture of it?

527
00:31:33,800 --> 00:31:38,800
So, what you did with those, they were really cheap.

528
00:31:38,800 --> 00:31:40,800
You could buy one in 1820.

529
00:31:40,800 --> 00:31:43,800
Go to a hardware store in downtown New Haven.

530
00:31:43,800 --> 00:31:45,800
They'd have a whole set of them.

531
00:31:45,800 --> 00:31:47,800
And you use it to make holes.

532
00:31:47,800 --> 00:31:49,800
It works really well.

533
00:31:49,800 --> 00:31:51,800
I find you can buy them on the Internet.

534
00:31:51,800 --> 00:31:52,800
I bought one out of curiosity.

535
00:31:52,800 --> 00:31:53,800
And now I use it.

536
00:31:53,800 --> 00:31:57,800
The point is that inventions come and go.

537
00:31:57,800 --> 00:32:02,800
The nice thing about a gimlet is, it's really nice.

538
00:32:02,800 --> 00:32:04,800
It comes to a pinpoint.

539
00:32:04,800 --> 00:32:06,800
And if you want to make a hole in wood,

540
00:32:06,800 --> 00:32:11,800
you just push it in, and it goes exactly where you want it.

541
00:32:11,800 --> 00:32:14,800
And then you just turn it a little bit, and it goes right in.

542
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:18,800
Well, we have electric drills now, right?

543
00:32:18,800 --> 00:32:21,800
We're kind of used to the problem.

544
00:32:21,800 --> 00:32:23,800
Once you start using a gimlet, you love it.

545
00:32:23,800 --> 00:32:26,800
Because you can control it so well.

546
00:32:26,800 --> 00:32:29,800
That drill, when you start drilling, it kind of bounces around,

547
00:32:29,800 --> 00:32:31,800
and it doesn't make the hole where you want it.

548
00:32:31,800 --> 00:32:36,800
So, there's something good about this invention.

549
00:32:36,800 --> 00:32:41,800
You can go out and buy one now for very little money on the Internet.

550
00:32:41,800 --> 00:32:42,800
It'll be mailed to you.

551
00:32:42,800 --> 00:32:46,800
Now that I have it, I'm thinking about it all the time, for using it.

552
00:32:46,800 --> 00:32:48,800
But somehow, it comes and goes.

553
00:32:48,800 --> 00:32:54,800
Inventions are part of our culture that appear and disappear through time.

554
00:32:54,800 --> 00:32:55,800
So, thank you.

555
00:32:55,800 --> 00:32:56,800
Thank you.

556
00:32:56,800 --> 00:33:02,800
I wanted to talk about another invention.

557
00:33:02,800 --> 00:33:04,800
This is by way of inspiration.

558
00:33:04,800 --> 00:33:06,800
But I'm getting to finance in a minute.

559
00:33:06,800 --> 00:33:09,800
But I have to do these simple –

560
00:33:09,800 --> 00:33:11,800
maybe someone will reintroduce the gimlet.

561
00:33:11,800 --> 00:33:12,800
I don't know.

562
00:33:12,800 --> 00:33:14,800
It seems like it's probably gone forever.

563
00:33:14,800 --> 00:33:17,800
It illustrates the fact that modern electronic technology

564
00:33:17,800 --> 00:33:20,800
has given us something maybe better.

565
00:33:20,800 --> 00:33:23,800
But if you were used to using this, you'd want it.

566
00:33:23,800 --> 00:33:25,800
I just don't want to use that electric drill,

567
00:33:25,800 --> 00:33:30,800
because I know how to use this, and I can control it.

568
00:33:30,800 --> 00:33:35,800
I'll give you another example of an important invention,

569
00:33:35,800 --> 00:33:38,800
which I saw come in my lifetime.

570
00:33:38,800 --> 00:33:43,800
And that's wheeled suitcases.

571
00:33:43,800 --> 00:33:44,800
Okay.

572
00:33:44,800 --> 00:33:46,800
I bet you have one of these, right?

573
00:33:46,800 --> 00:33:49,800
A suitcase with wheels on it.

574
00:33:49,800 --> 00:33:53,800
When I was a boy, there were absolutely none.

575
00:33:53,800 --> 00:33:54,800
Nowhere.

576
00:33:54,800 --> 00:33:57,800
No one had a wheeled suitcase.

577
00:33:57,800 --> 00:33:59,800
Isn't that strange?

578
00:33:59,800 --> 00:34:01,800
You'd be carrying these suitcases around,

579
00:34:01,800 --> 00:34:04,800
when you put it on wheels.

580
00:34:04,800 --> 00:34:09,800
So, I looked up the inventors of this.

581
00:34:09,800 --> 00:34:11,800
It's not that long ago.

582
00:34:11,800 --> 00:34:15,800
I thought I – oh, yeah, okay.

583
00:34:15,800 --> 00:34:23,800
It was – the wheeled suitcase was invented by Bernard Seydal.

584
00:34:24,800 --> 00:34:32,800
In 1972.

585
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:34,800
And his was the first one.

586
00:34:34,800 --> 00:34:37,800
He had a suitcase with little wheels, four little wheels.

587
00:34:37,800 --> 00:34:39,800
And you had a strap, and you'd pull it,

588
00:34:39,800 --> 00:34:41,800
and it would trail behind you.

589
00:34:41,800 --> 00:34:44,800
But then the other, the really important invention

590
00:34:44,800 --> 00:34:47,800
was by Robert Clath.

591
00:34:47,800 --> 00:34:50,800
This isn't finance, but I kind of think of it as finance.

592
00:34:50,800 --> 00:34:52,800
In –

593
00:34:52,800 --> 00:34:55,800
it was 1991.

594
00:34:55,800 --> 00:34:57,800
And he called it the roller board.

595
00:34:57,800 --> 00:34:58,800
This is what you own, right?

596
00:34:58,800 --> 00:35:00,800
You own a roller board.

597
00:35:00,800 --> 00:35:01,800
Right?

598
00:35:01,800 --> 00:35:05,800
It has a rigid handle that collapses into the suitcase,

599
00:35:05,800 --> 00:35:07,800
and it has two wheels that are horizontal.

600
00:35:07,800 --> 00:35:10,800
The problem with Bernard Seydal's invention

601
00:35:10,800 --> 00:35:12,800
is that it tended to flop over.

602
00:35:12,800 --> 00:35:15,800
You're pulling it with a strap behind you.

603
00:35:15,800 --> 00:35:17,800
And then you look back, and it's flopped over.

604
00:35:17,800 --> 00:35:20,800
You can't go around corners very well.

605
00:35:20,800 --> 00:35:24,800
So, we lived with Seydals for 19 years.

606
00:35:24,800 --> 00:35:29,800
And it took 19 years to invent the modern roller board.

607
00:35:29,800 --> 00:35:33,800
So, I actually heard from Bernard Seydal.

608
00:35:33,800 --> 00:35:35,800
I wrote about him in New Financial Order.

609
00:35:35,800 --> 00:35:38,800
And I got a letter from him just about two weeks ago,

610
00:35:38,800 --> 00:35:40,800
or email from him.

611
00:35:40,800 --> 00:35:42,800
And he said, I heard about your book.

612
00:35:42,800 --> 00:35:45,800
And he thought you'd talk about me in your book.

613
00:35:45,800 --> 00:35:49,800
And he said he'd like to get me to autograph it for him.

614
00:35:49,800 --> 00:35:52,800
So, I said, fine, I'll send you the book.

615
00:35:52,800 --> 00:35:55,800
And I sent him an autograph with appreciation of it.

616
00:35:55,800 --> 00:35:57,800
But that just shows it's not long ago.

617
00:35:57,800 --> 00:36:00,800
So, I guess I could ask you to reflect,

618
00:36:00,800 --> 00:36:04,800
why didn't people have wheeled suitcases?

619
00:36:04,800 --> 00:36:06,800
But you weren't born yet, right?

620
00:36:06,800 --> 00:36:08,800
So, I can't reflect on that.

621
00:36:08,800 --> 00:36:10,800
I'm thinking back to myself.

622
00:36:10,800 --> 00:36:12,800
You know, it's almost something that I would do myself.

623
00:36:12,800 --> 00:36:14,800
I'd put wheels on it.

624
00:36:14,800 --> 00:36:16,800
But I guess you were embarrassed to do it back then.

625
00:36:16,800 --> 00:36:18,800
Because maybe it seems sissy to do it.

626
00:36:19,800 --> 00:36:21,800
But was everybody worried about being a sissy?

627
00:36:21,800 --> 00:36:22,800
Not everybody.

628
00:36:22,800 --> 00:36:24,800
Why doesn't anybody do it?

629
00:36:24,800 --> 00:36:27,800
Well, Bernard Seda, I had my student interview him.

630
00:36:27,800 --> 00:36:29,800
And he listened to objections.

631
00:36:29,800 --> 00:36:33,800
And people told him in 1972, no one's going to buy that.

632
00:36:33,800 --> 00:36:39,800
Because if the suitcase is too heavy, you just get a red cap.

633
00:36:39,800 --> 00:36:41,800
You know what a red cap is?

634
00:36:41,800 --> 00:36:43,800
You know what a red cap is?

635
00:36:43,800 --> 00:36:46,800
A red cap is someone, a poorly paid person,

636
00:36:46,800 --> 00:36:48,800
who stands around at train stations,

637
00:36:48,800 --> 00:36:50,800
and he's wearing a red cap,

638
00:36:50,800 --> 00:36:52,800
and helps you with your luggage.

639
00:36:52,800 --> 00:36:54,800
That's not a good answer, right?

640
00:36:54,800 --> 00:36:56,800
There isn't always a red cap to help you.

641
00:36:56,800 --> 00:37:00,800
And so, obviously, you need wheels on suitcases.

642
00:37:00,800 --> 00:37:02,800
But this illustrates how technology moves.

643
00:37:02,800 --> 00:37:05,800
It moves sometimes slowly.

644
00:37:05,800 --> 00:37:08,800
I'll give you a couple other examples from my book

645
00:37:08,800 --> 00:37:10,800
about the slowness of inventions.

646
00:37:10,800 --> 00:37:12,800
One is just the invention of wheels.

647
00:37:12,800 --> 00:37:16,800
Did you know that in the Americas, before Columbus,

648
00:37:16,800 --> 00:37:18,800
there were no wheels?

649
00:37:18,800 --> 00:37:23,800
The American Indians had no wheeled vehicles at all.

650
00:37:23,800 --> 00:37:25,800
Not even suitcases, nothing.

651
00:37:25,800 --> 00:37:27,800
No wagons, nothing.

652
00:37:27,800 --> 00:37:30,800
And then to complicate it, they've discovered,

653
00:37:30,800 --> 00:37:33,800
in the late classical period in Mexico,

654
00:37:33,800 --> 00:37:35,800
they've discovered wheeled toys.

655
00:37:35,800 --> 00:37:39,800
And if you go to a museum in Mexico, you can see them.

656
00:37:39,800 --> 00:37:41,800
They made toys for their kids.

657
00:37:41,800 --> 00:37:42,800
They're not cars.

658
00:37:42,800 --> 00:37:44,800
They're little jaguars and animals.

659
00:37:44,800 --> 00:37:46,800
And you could roll them along the floor.

660
00:37:46,800 --> 00:37:47,800
So they made the toys.

661
00:37:47,800 --> 00:37:50,800
And then they thought, why didn't they think of making a wagon?

662
00:37:50,800 --> 00:37:53,800
It just never occurred to them.

663
00:37:53,800 --> 00:37:56,800
And another example I give in my book that I really like

664
00:37:56,800 --> 00:37:58,800
is the movie subtitle.

665
00:37:58,800 --> 00:38:00,800
This is an amazing thing.

666
00:38:00,800 --> 00:38:04,800
When they invented movies, I gave you a Thomas Edison sound movie,

667
00:38:04,800 --> 00:38:05,800
but it didn't work.

668
00:38:05,800 --> 00:38:07,800
He couldn't make a sound movie.

669
00:38:07,800 --> 00:38:10,800
They didn't find out how to make sound movies until the 1920s.

670
00:38:10,800 --> 00:38:13,800
So they did what they, in order to give dialogue to a movie,

671
00:38:13,800 --> 00:38:15,800
they'd give what they called intertitles.

672
00:38:15,800 --> 00:38:16,800
They would show the movie.

673
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:18,800
And then, do you know what I'm saying?

674
00:38:18,800 --> 00:38:22,800
Then they'd stop the movie, and there'd be a title with some phrase that someone said.

675
00:38:22,800 --> 00:38:26,800
And then it would go back to the movie.

676
00:38:26,800 --> 00:38:30,800
But it's obviously better just to put subtitles on the bottom of the screen, right?

677
00:38:30,800 --> 00:38:32,800
Let the movie proceed.

678
00:38:32,800 --> 00:38:34,800
You've seen movies with subtitles, right?

679
00:38:34,800 --> 00:38:35,800
It works fine.

680
00:38:35,800 --> 00:38:38,800
Well, it turns out that someone tried it in 1920.

681
00:38:38,800 --> 00:38:41,800
There was a movie called The Chamber Mystery.

682
00:38:41,800 --> 00:38:44,800
And someone made a subtitled silent movie.

683
00:38:44,800 --> 00:38:47,800
But the response was bad.

684
00:38:47,800 --> 00:38:49,800
It seemed like nobody liked it.

685
00:38:49,800 --> 00:38:51,800
And so it wasn't for another.

686
00:38:51,800 --> 00:38:55,800
Subtitles didn't come in until after sound movies were invented.

687
00:38:55,800 --> 00:39:00,800
After sound movies were invented, they wanted to make movies in one language

688
00:39:00,800 --> 00:39:02,800
and show them in another country.

689
00:39:02,800 --> 00:39:06,800
So they had to put subtitles in so that people in another country could see the movie.

690
00:39:06,800 --> 00:39:08,800
And then that's how it came in.

691
00:39:08,800 --> 00:39:13,800
There's a slowness to understand or appreciate invention.

692
00:39:13,800 --> 00:39:17,800
Anyway, I want to move to invention in finance.

693
00:39:17,800 --> 00:39:25,800
And that's the analogies that I've just talked about are important.

694
00:39:25,800 --> 00:39:36,800
So let me start with an important invention in finance that goes way back.

695
00:39:36,800 --> 00:39:37,800
Well, I was going to start.

696
00:39:37,800 --> 00:39:42,800
I think I mentioned last time the very simple idea of setting up a company and dividing

697
00:39:42,800 --> 00:39:45,800
up shares in the company.

698
00:39:45,800 --> 00:39:46,800
That's a really old invention.

699
00:39:46,800 --> 00:39:48,800
That's thousands of years old.

700
00:39:48,800 --> 00:39:52,800
All right, you and your friends are going to do a business.

701
00:39:52,800 --> 00:39:54,800
How do we divide up the profits?

702
00:39:54,800 --> 00:39:57,800
Well, let's give shares to each of us.

703
00:39:57,800 --> 00:40:00,800
And the guy who's contributing more gets more shares, right?

704
00:40:00,800 --> 00:40:03,800
And someone who's contributing less gets less shares.

705
00:40:03,800 --> 00:40:06,800
But then we all have an incentive to make the company go.

706
00:40:06,800 --> 00:40:11,800
And that's the idea of a corporation.

707
00:40:11,800 --> 00:40:19,800
A corporation comes from the Latin word corpus, which is a body.

708
00:40:19,800 --> 00:40:26,800
It becomes like a slave owned mutually by all the people in the company.

709
00:40:26,800 --> 00:40:31,800
And your share in the company is determined by the number of shares that you own.

710
00:40:31,800 --> 00:40:32,800
But I wanted to focus here.

711
00:40:32,800 --> 00:40:33,800
That's an old idea.

712
00:40:33,800 --> 00:40:34,800
It's an invention.

713
00:40:34,800 --> 00:40:39,800
But I wanted to really focus on a particular nuance that developed.

714
00:40:39,800 --> 00:40:41,800
It's called corporation.

715
00:40:41,800 --> 00:40:54,800
It's limited liability.

716
00:40:54,800 --> 00:41:04,800
A limited liability corporation is a corporation that guarantees that you as a shareholder

717
00:41:04,800 --> 00:41:08,800
will not be liable for the debts of the company.

718
00:41:08,800 --> 00:41:11,800
In England, they sometimes will put limited.

719
00:41:11,800 --> 00:41:16,800
So, you can put the name of the company and after it will say limited.

720
00:41:16,800 --> 00:41:19,800
But in the U.S., we just say incorporated.

721
00:41:19,800 --> 00:41:24,800
So, that tells you that it's limited liability.

722
00:41:24,800 --> 00:41:32,800
What it's talking about, limited liability means that somebody sues the company and the company can't pay.

723
00:41:32,800 --> 00:41:36,800
They can't go after the stockholders.

724
00:41:36,800 --> 00:41:40,800
So, limited liability has kind of a complicated history.

725
00:41:40,800 --> 00:41:49,800
According to a history by David Moss, who is a Yale history graduate student when he wrote this,

726
00:41:49,800 --> 00:41:59,800
but is now at the Harvard Business School, it started, limited liability really took hold in 1811

727
00:41:59,800 --> 00:42:09,800
with a corporate law in the state of New York, which represented a significant invention.

728
00:42:10,800 --> 00:42:17,800
The corporate law of New York in 1811 actually had two important components.

729
00:42:17,800 --> 00:42:22,800
The first component was anybody can start a corporation.

730
00:42:22,800 --> 00:42:29,800
Just file the papers with the government of New York and you've got a corporation.

731
00:42:29,800 --> 00:42:32,800
There may have been some regulatory requirements.

732
00:42:32,800 --> 00:42:38,800
But the point was, you didn't need an act of Congress or an act of Parliament to start a corporation.

733
00:42:38,800 --> 00:42:39,800
It used to be limited liability.

734
00:42:39,800 --> 00:42:42,800
It used to be very hard to start a company.

735
00:42:42,800 --> 00:42:45,800
Because people would say, well, what's your purpose?

736
00:42:45,800 --> 00:42:48,800
And, you know, we don't just do this automatically.

737
00:42:48,800 --> 00:42:52,800
That was the first part of the New York law in 1811.

738
00:42:52,800 --> 00:42:55,800
But the second part is particularly interesting.

739
00:42:55,800 --> 00:43:01,800
They said, under no circumstances can the shareholders be sued.

740
00:43:01,800 --> 00:43:04,800
You put in your money to the company, that's it.

741
00:43:04,800 --> 00:43:06,800
You are protected by the law.

742
00:43:06,800 --> 00:43:08,800
No worries about that.

743
00:43:09,800 --> 00:43:20,800
Now, this was the first corporate law in the world, according to Moss, that imposed limited liability as a clear right of the shareholder.

744
00:43:20,800 --> 00:43:26,800
There were limited liability clauses before, but it was never so crystal clear.

745
00:43:26,800 --> 00:43:35,800
At the same time, around 1811, other states in the United States were looking at New York and saying, you're crazy.

746
00:43:35,800 --> 00:43:36,800
What are you doing?

747
00:43:36,800 --> 00:43:38,800
You can't sue the shareholders?

748
00:43:38,800 --> 00:43:42,800
They could do something irresponsible.

749
00:43:42,800 --> 00:43:48,800
And so, the state of Massachusetts at around the same year made a completely opposite law.

750
00:43:48,800 --> 00:43:52,800
And they said, they made it clear that the shareholders are responsible.

751
00:43:52,800 --> 00:43:55,800
You invest in a company, you're responsible.

752
00:43:55,800 --> 00:43:58,800
And that's the only way it's fair, they thought.

753
00:43:58,800 --> 00:44:00,800
Well, guess what happened?

754
00:44:00,800 --> 00:44:03,800
New York became the financial center of America.

755
00:44:03,800 --> 00:44:07,800
Nobody wanted to set up a company in Massachusetts anymore.

756
00:44:07,800 --> 00:44:10,800
Because they couldn't raise money.

757
00:44:10,800 --> 00:44:15,800
The capitalists were at risk.

758
00:44:15,800 --> 00:44:24,800
If you bought one share in a company in Massachusetts, and the company did something criminal, let's say, or bad, I don't know, something bad,

759
00:44:24,800 --> 00:44:30,800
then they might sue the company and then come looking for anybody who is among the shareholders.

760
00:44:30,800 --> 00:44:34,800
You own one share in the company, so we can take everything from you.

761
00:44:34,800 --> 00:44:35,800
We can take your house, your car.

762
00:44:35,800 --> 00:44:36,800
Well, you didn't have a car.

763
00:44:36,800 --> 00:44:38,800
You take whatever you have.

764
00:44:38,800 --> 00:44:42,800
So, the rule was, be really careful before you invest in any company.

765
00:44:42,800 --> 00:44:44,800
And you've got to watch them all the time.

766
00:44:44,800 --> 00:44:46,800
But you know what that did?

767
00:44:46,800 --> 00:44:48,800
That kept Massachusetts down.

768
00:44:48,800 --> 00:44:50,800
Because nobody wanted to do that.

769
00:44:50,800 --> 00:44:53,800
I mean, you'd only invest with trusted friends.

770
00:44:53,800 --> 00:44:55,800
You'd never invest in just some random company.

771
00:44:55,800 --> 00:45:00,800
But what happened in New York was that companies started appearing rapidly.

772
00:45:00,800 --> 00:45:02,800
Because of this.

773
00:45:02,800 --> 00:45:06,800
And as many critics said, a lot of companies,

774
00:45:06,800 --> 00:45:08,800
a lot of these companies are fly-by-night.

775
00:45:08,800 --> 00:45:09,800
They're going to go under.

776
00:45:09,800 --> 00:45:12,800
We're going to find out that they weren't doing a good business.

777
00:45:12,800 --> 00:45:16,800
They were just show-offs, and they go bankrupt.

778
00:45:16,800 --> 00:45:19,800
But some of those companies did extremely well.

779
00:45:19,800 --> 00:45:24,800
And they created a powerful New York economy.

780
00:45:24,800 --> 00:45:30,800
And so, then people learned, well, yeah, you could invest in 100 companies, and 99 of them will go under.

781
00:45:30,800 --> 00:45:32,800
They're all wild.

782
00:45:32,800 --> 00:45:33,800
Not all of them, though.

783
00:45:33,800 --> 00:45:35,800
There's one that will be the Walmart.

784
00:45:35,800 --> 00:45:39,800
And it's going to make you rich.

785
00:45:39,800 --> 00:45:47,800
So, the example set by New York was eventually copied by Massachusetts, by every state in the United States,

786
00:45:47,800 --> 00:45:51,800
by every country in Europe, and now every country in the world.

787
00:45:51,800 --> 00:45:58,800
See, it was an experiment that might not have gone well.

788
00:45:58,800 --> 00:46:00,800
You know what David Moss thought?

789
00:46:00,800 --> 00:46:03,800
And this has to do with framing, again.

790
00:46:03,800 --> 00:46:07,800
Why is it that New York became such a capital of finance?

791
00:46:07,800 --> 00:46:09,800
Because of this law.

792
00:46:09,800 --> 00:46:12,800
And because it made investing fun.

793
00:46:12,800 --> 00:46:14,800
And this is a matter of framing.

794
00:46:14,800 --> 00:46:21,800
Moss emphasized that when you buy a share in a company, and it's limited liability,

795
00:46:21,800 --> 00:46:26,800
you know that you've already put out all the money that you'll ever put out.

796
00:46:26,800 --> 00:46:28,800
But you could get an infinite amount.

797
00:46:28,800 --> 00:46:31,800
Well, there's no limit to the amount of money on the upside.

798
00:46:31,800 --> 00:46:32,800
And he thought, that's not the way to do it.

799
00:46:33,800 --> 00:46:39,800
And he said, it's just framed better as a psychologically appealing gamble.

800
00:46:39,800 --> 00:46:41,800
It's like a lottery ticket.

801
00:46:41,800 --> 00:46:43,800
People like lottery tickets, right?

802
00:46:43,800 --> 00:46:45,800
They just enjoy the thought.

803
00:46:45,800 --> 00:46:48,800
I don't know, I don't do this, but apparently a lot of people do this.

804
00:46:48,800 --> 00:46:52,800
You go and you spend a dollar for a lottery, or two dollars for a lottery ticket,

805
00:46:52,800 --> 00:46:56,800
and then you think, tonight I could be a millionaire.

806
00:46:56,800 --> 00:46:57,800
Alright?

807
00:46:57,800 --> 00:47:01,800
And so, that's just so much fun that you want to do it again.

808
00:47:01,800 --> 00:47:03,800
But if the lottery ticket said,

809
00:47:03,800 --> 00:47:06,800
well, you have a small probability of being a millionaire,

810
00:47:06,800 --> 00:47:09,800
but you also have a small probability of going bankrupt.

811
00:47:09,800 --> 00:47:11,800
We're going to come after you and take everything.

812
00:47:11,800 --> 00:47:12,800
You wouldn't like that.

813
00:47:12,800 --> 00:47:14,800
It wouldn't be so much fun, right?

814
00:47:14,800 --> 00:47:19,800
You like to savor the possibility of getting rich.

815
00:47:19,800 --> 00:47:22,800
And so, people flock to these stocks.

816
00:47:22,800 --> 00:47:24,800
It's like a sport.

817
00:47:24,800 --> 00:47:29,800
It's an invention of something fun to do for a lot of people.

818
00:47:29,800 --> 00:47:31,800
But it has this productive side to it,

819
00:47:31,800 --> 00:47:35,800
that it funnels capital to where it's needed.

820
00:47:35,800 --> 00:47:39,800
So, that was an important invention.

821
00:47:39,800 --> 00:47:47,800
I want to give you another example that is a variation on this.

822
00:47:47,800 --> 00:47:49,800
I'm going to move forward in time.

823
00:47:49,800 --> 00:47:53,800
My next invention is the,

824
00:47:53,800 --> 00:47:57,800
oh, God, I'm breaking chalk here.

825
00:47:57,800 --> 00:48:00,800
This goes now to China.

826
00:48:00,800 --> 00:48:15,800
This is the Township and Village Enterprise, or PVE.

827
00:48:15,800 --> 00:48:18,800
And I'll try to say it in Chinese,

828
00:48:18,800 --> 00:48:21,800
.

829
00:48:21,800 --> 00:48:23,800
Did I say that right?

830
00:48:23,800 --> 00:48:26,800
Close, anyway.

831
00:48:26,800 --> 00:48:28,800
So, what was this?

832
00:48:28,800 --> 00:48:34,800
When China emerged from a strictly communist state,

833
00:48:34,800 --> 00:48:40,800
in the early period, maybe starting in the late 70s,

834
00:48:40,800 --> 00:48:43,800
but more in the 1980s,

835
00:48:43,800 --> 00:48:49,800
the Chinese economy increasingly became built on a certain kind of organization

836
00:48:49,800 --> 00:48:53,800
called a township or TVE.

837
00:48:53,800 --> 00:48:57,800
And the TVE was like a company, a corporation,

838
00:48:57,800 --> 00:49:02,800
except that it always involved the town.

839
00:49:02,800 --> 00:49:05,800
So, in other words, if you wanted to start a business,

840
00:49:05,800 --> 00:49:09,800
making something, making toys for export to the world,

841
00:49:09,800 --> 00:49:12,800
you wouldn't just start a toy company.

842
00:49:12,800 --> 00:49:15,800
You would go to the mayor of your town,

843
00:49:15,800 --> 00:49:18,800
and you'd talk to the mayor and say,

844
00:49:18,800 --> 00:49:22,800
I want to start a toy company as this town's enterprise.

845
00:49:22,800 --> 00:49:26,800
And I want to share the profits with you, with the whole town.

846
00:49:26,800 --> 00:49:29,800
Now, this is kind of unique.

847
00:49:29,800 --> 00:49:33,800
I don't know if it's unique, but we don't see this in the United States.

848
00:49:33,800 --> 00:49:35,800
But it proliferated.

849
00:49:35,800 --> 00:49:42,800
It was actually the invention that led the initial successes of the Chinese economy.

850
00:49:42,800 --> 00:49:47,800
By 1985, I believe, I have the statistics here,

851
00:49:47,800 --> 00:49:52,800
there were 12 million TVEs in China.

852
00:49:52,800 --> 00:49:54,800
By the mid-1990s,

853
00:49:54,800 --> 00:50:01,800
most of the industrial production of China was done by TVEs.

854
00:50:01,800 --> 00:50:03,800
So, then you have to ask, why?

855
00:50:03,800 --> 00:50:07,800
This is an invention, but we don't see it in other countries.

856
00:50:07,800 --> 00:50:10,800
Why in China?

857
00:50:10,800 --> 00:50:17,800
Well, people who look back on it think that it was inventing around certain constraints

858
00:50:17,800 --> 00:50:19,800
at the time in China.

859
00:50:19,800 --> 00:50:23,800
And the invention got around a legal constraint,

860
00:50:23,800 --> 00:50:27,800
that China, having been a communist country,

861
00:50:27,800 --> 00:50:31,800
did not have all these financial lawyers.

862
00:50:31,800 --> 00:50:36,800
And they did not have courts that enforced legal contracts,

863
00:50:36,800 --> 00:50:38,800
the way we do in the U.S.

864
00:50:38,800 --> 00:50:45,800
And that entrepreneurs in China were inhibited from starting an enterprise,

865
00:50:45,800 --> 00:50:49,800
because they thought it would just be usurped by the village.

866
00:50:49,800 --> 00:50:52,800
You live in a village, you start a toy company,

867
00:50:52,800 --> 00:50:54,800
as soon as you start making money,

868
00:50:54,800 --> 00:50:58,800
they'll just put a tax on you, or they'll take it.

869
00:50:58,800 --> 00:51:00,800
That was your worry.

870
00:51:00,800 --> 00:51:02,800
So, you had to involve them.

871
00:51:02,800 --> 00:51:06,800
It was a fact of life, very important fact of life.

872
00:51:06,800 --> 00:51:09,800
You had to involve the whole community.

873
00:51:09,800 --> 00:51:11,800
You could not start a business.

874
00:51:11,800 --> 00:51:16,800
You could not start a business without involving the community.

875
00:51:16,800 --> 00:51:19,800
But it was a very successful invention,

876
00:51:19,800 --> 00:51:21,800
because it actually led,

877
00:51:22,800 --> 00:51:28,800
it actually led the Chinese economy

878
00:51:28,800 --> 00:51:33,800
on its first huge successes.

879
00:51:33,800 --> 00:51:36,800
Okay, I'm going to move to another example now

880
00:51:36,800 --> 00:51:39,800
of an innovation in finance.

881
00:51:39,800 --> 00:51:48,800
And I want to talk about inflation indexation.

882
00:51:48,800 --> 00:51:50,800
.

883
00:51:50,800 --> 00:51:56,800
And I could pick many.

884
00:51:56,800 --> 00:51:59,800
These are examples that I like personally.

885
00:51:59,800 --> 00:52:01,800
There's an infinite number of examples.

886
00:52:01,800 --> 00:52:04,800
It doesn't matter exactly which example we pick.

887
00:52:04,800 --> 00:52:10,800
But there's a fundamental financial problem,

888
00:52:10,800 --> 00:52:14,800
and that is that the value of money changes through time.

889
00:52:14,800 --> 00:52:18,800
We have inflation measured by a consumer price index.

890
00:52:18,800 --> 00:52:22,800
And when inflation proceeds, the prices go up,

891
00:52:22,800 --> 00:52:25,800
and so the buying power of money goes down.

892
00:52:25,800 --> 00:52:27,800
And it's uncertain.

893
00:52:27,800 --> 00:52:39,800
There's inflation risk that we face because of the uncertainty about prices.

894
00:52:39,800 --> 00:52:43,800
This is an inflation risk that's under concern right now

895
00:52:43,800 --> 00:52:46,800
after the quantitative easing that the Fed,

896
00:52:46,800 --> 00:52:47,800
we'll come back to that.

897
00:52:47,800 --> 00:52:53,800
The Federal Reserve is expanding the money supply dramatically

898
00:52:53,800 --> 00:52:55,800
in an effort to deal with this crisis.

899
00:52:55,800 --> 00:52:58,800
And some people are worried about inflation risk.

900
00:52:58,800 --> 00:53:00,800
But it's a long-standing worry.

901
00:53:00,800 --> 00:53:04,800
It goes way back in time.

902
00:53:04,800 --> 00:53:11,800
Most debts are done in nominal terms.

903
00:53:11,800 --> 00:53:16,800
.

904
00:53:16,800 --> 00:53:23,800
And that means they're written in the currency units.

905
00:53:23,800 --> 00:53:27,800
And if the currency becomes worthless, you're wiped out.

906
00:53:27,800 --> 00:53:31,800
Why do people write contracts in currency units?

907
00:53:31,800 --> 00:53:35,800
Well, it's because it's familiar with them, and so it's framing.

908
00:53:35,800 --> 00:53:38,800
We're used to thinking in terms of money,

909
00:53:38,800 --> 00:53:42,800
and so we write contracts in terms of money all the time.

910
00:53:42,800 --> 00:53:44,800
.

911
00:53:44,800 --> 00:53:49,800
So, the idea is, maybe we should start something else.

912
00:53:49,800 --> 00:53:51,800
Let's not write contracts in terms of money.

913
00:53:51,800 --> 00:53:54,800
Let's write in terms of something else.

914
00:53:54,800 --> 00:53:56,800
And that's an invention.

915
00:53:56,800 --> 00:53:58,800
But it's another invention.

916
00:53:58,800 --> 00:54:00,800
It seems obvious.

917
00:54:00,800 --> 00:54:05,800
Let's index our debts to some measure of inflation.

918
00:54:05,800 --> 00:54:12,800
So, the invention I want to talk about first goes back to 1780.

919
00:54:12,800 --> 00:54:22,800
I believe that the first indexed bond was issued in the state of Massachusetts in 1780

920
00:54:22,800 --> 00:54:28,800
by the Massachusetts government.

921
00:54:28,800 --> 00:54:35,800
And that was an indexed bond.

922
00:54:35,800 --> 00:54:38,800
They defined a consumer price index.

923
00:54:38,800 --> 00:54:40,800
They didn't call it that.

924
00:54:42,800 --> 00:54:45,800
They called it the British pound.

925
00:54:45,800 --> 00:54:49,800
And they said that the bond would pay you so many pounds.

926
00:54:49,800 --> 00:54:50,800
They weren't using dollars yet.

927
00:54:50,800 --> 00:54:54,800
They were using pounds, British pounds.

928
00:54:54,800 --> 00:55:00,800
The amount of pounds you would get would be increased if inflation were to start.

929
00:55:00,800 --> 00:55:05,800
So, they defined an index of commodities that you might purchase.

930
00:55:05,800 --> 00:55:11,800
And that index was used to input a formula

931
00:55:11,800 --> 00:55:16,800
that created inflation correction to the bonds.

932
00:55:16,800 --> 00:55:21,800
So, these bonds were issued in 1780.

933
00:55:21,800 --> 00:55:28,800
And it served an important purpose because the U.S. was fighting a war with the United Kingdom at the time.

934
00:55:28,800 --> 00:55:30,800
And there was tremendous inflation.

935
00:55:30,800 --> 00:55:32,800
So, they were very important.

936
00:55:32,800 --> 00:55:36,800
But after the war, after the Revolutionary War ended,

937
00:55:36,800 --> 00:55:38,800
they stopped issuing these bonds.

938
00:55:38,800 --> 00:55:40,800
And they forgot about them in the United States.

939
00:55:40,800 --> 00:55:44,800
Until 1997.

940
00:55:44,800 --> 00:55:46,800
It's amazing.

941
00:55:46,800 --> 00:55:50,800
Why did the invention disappear?

942
00:55:50,800 --> 00:55:52,800
It's like gimlets.

943
00:55:52,800 --> 00:55:54,800
I think maybe gimlets will make a reappearance.

944
00:55:54,800 --> 00:55:56,800
Maybe some of you will buy a gimlet.

945
00:55:56,800 --> 00:55:58,800
They're very cheap, by the way.

946
00:55:58,800 --> 00:56:01,800
And they're very useful.

947
00:56:01,800 --> 00:56:09,800
So, maybe, it seems like there's a psychological barrier toward adopting something that's not a good thing.

948
00:56:09,800 --> 00:56:13,800
But they're adopting something that's a little bit complicated.

949
00:56:13,800 --> 00:56:17,800
And people have trouble understanding index bonds.

950
00:56:17,800 --> 00:56:29,800
And they have trouble understanding how the formula works.

951
00:56:29,800 --> 00:56:31,800
And they think, you know, I just want money.

952
00:56:31,800 --> 00:56:33,800
I just want money.

953
00:56:33,800 --> 00:56:37,800
But then you ask, well, why didn't people learn that?

954
00:56:37,800 --> 00:56:39,800
And you know, it's the same thing, isn't it?

955
00:56:39,800 --> 00:56:43,800
If you look through history, we've had bouts of inflation so many times in so many different countries.

956
00:56:43,800 --> 00:56:49,800
And, you know, if you buy a nominal bond, it's risky, especially if you buy a 30-year bond.

957
00:56:49,800 --> 00:56:51,800
People are doing that, even today, all over the world.

958
00:56:51,800 --> 00:56:57,800
They buy these 30-year bonds, denominated in dollars or euros or some other currency.

959
00:56:57,800 --> 00:56:59,800
But what's going to happen to those currencies?

960
00:56:59,800 --> 00:57:04,800
So, why don't you denominate your bond in something more realistic?

961
00:57:04,800 --> 00:57:09,400
Well, after 1997, the introduction in the United

962
00:57:09,400 --> 00:57:13,540
States of index bonds, the reintroduction in 1997, it

963
00:57:13,540 --> 00:57:17,840
seemed to be started by one person, Larry Summers, who was

964
00:57:17,840 --> 00:57:20,200
Assistant Treasury Secretary.

965
00:57:20,200 --> 00:57:23,020
Just believed in this, and he did it.

966
00:57:23,020 --> 00:57:24,200
And so, we still have them.

967
00:57:24,200 --> 00:57:28,460
It got up to over 10% of the U.S. debt.

968
00:57:28,460 --> 00:57:33,940
Now, under Bush and Obama, they're kind of letting them

969
00:57:33,940 --> 00:57:38,700
sag now, and they're down to 6% of the U.S. debt.

970
00:57:38,700 --> 00:57:41,180
But it really should be 100% of the U.S. debt

971
00:57:41,180 --> 00:57:42,400
should be indexed.

972
00:57:42,400 --> 00:57:46,200
So this is a kind of example of progress that is made

973
00:57:46,200 --> 00:57:47,280
gradually through time.

974
00:57:47,280 --> 00:57:52,240
And maybe you'll see that in the next 40 years.

975
00:57:52,240 --> 00:57:55,260
I'm trying to think of how we can make these things happen.

976
00:57:55,260 --> 00:58:02,180
That's an agenda for younger people who can start

977
00:58:02,180 --> 00:58:03,560
innovating and changing our

978
00:58:03,560 --> 00:58:03,860
finances.

979
00:58:03,860 --> 00:58:03,920
Thank you.

980
00:58:03,940 --> 00:58:07,260
I think you're getting over to the financial system.

981
00:58:07,260 --> 00:58:14,980
So I wanted to talk about an invention that particularly

982
00:58:14,980 --> 00:58:21,320
intrigues me, that comes from the country of Chile.

983
00:58:21,320 --> 00:58:28,240
And it has to do with, it's a financial invention that

984
00:58:28,240 --> 00:58:33,860
overcomes framing problems.

985
00:58:33,860 --> 00:58:37,100
And it illustrates some of the basic concepts.

986
00:58:37,100 --> 00:58:39,420
So, let me talk about Chile.

987
00:58:39,420 --> 00:58:40,900
We're jumping around the world here.

988
00:58:43,620 --> 00:58:49,260
Chile had a problem with, like many Latin American

989
00:58:49,260 --> 00:58:53,260
countries, especially of that time, with inflation.

990
00:58:53,260 --> 00:59:00,580
So, they had a currency called the peso, which

991
00:59:00,580 --> 00:59:03,100
inflated enormously.

992
00:59:03,100 --> 00:59:05,740
It went up, I don't have the numbers on it, but it went up,

993
00:59:05,740 --> 00:59:10,620
you know, like prices went up a thousandfold.

994
00:59:10,620 --> 00:59:14,100
And people were saying, we can't trust anything.

995
00:59:14,100 --> 00:59:16,980
I wouldn't take a contract paying pesos.

996
00:59:16,980 --> 00:59:19,780
The peso is gradually becoming worthless.

997
00:59:19,780 --> 00:59:26,980
So, Chile switched to another currency called the escudo in

998
00:59:26,980 --> 00:59:30,340
1960.

999
00:59:30,340 --> 00:59:32,100
They said, OK, we're starting fresh.

1000
00:59:32,100 --> 00:59:33,060
We're starting fresh.

1001
00:59:33,060 --> 00:59:33,100
We're starting fresh.

1002
00:59:33,100 --> 00:59:34,700
We're not going to have inflation.

1003
00:59:34,700 --> 00:59:35,860
And this is brand new.

1004
00:59:35,860 --> 00:59:38,340
Now we're an escudo country, no inflation.

1005
00:59:41,100 --> 00:59:47,340
Then, in 1975, they switched back to the peso.

1006
00:59:47,340 --> 00:59:50,860
The exchange rate, peso of escudo, was, one escudo was

1007
00:59:50,860 --> 00:59:54,620
1,000 pesos, OK, because they had had something like a

1008
00:59:54,620 --> 00:59:56,820
thousandfold price increase.

1009
00:59:56,820 --> 01:00:00,020
And then, in 1975, the exchange rate was, one new

1010
01:00:00,020 --> 01:00:03,060
peso is 1,000 escudos.

1011
01:00:03,060 --> 01:00:06,740
They went up, a new peso was a million old pesos.

1012
01:00:06,740 --> 01:00:08,500
OK, this is getting embarrassing.

1013
01:00:08,500 --> 01:00:12,980
I mean, like, can we trust anything or anybody in Chile?

1014
01:00:12,980 --> 01:00:16,980
So, the invention started in 1967.

1015
01:00:16,980 --> 01:00:20,820
People in Chile were saying, we just want stability.

1016
01:00:20,820 --> 01:00:22,020
You know, this is crazy.

1017
01:00:22,020 --> 01:00:26,140
Our prices, if I own one peso, I have a nice thousand peso

1018
01:00:26,140 --> 01:00:31,980
note from 1955, and I pull it out in 1975, and it's worth

1019
01:00:31,980 --> 01:00:33,020
nothing.

1020
01:00:33,060 --> 01:00:35,740
It's not even worth the paper it's printed on.

1021
01:00:35,740 --> 01:00:38,260
So, we've got to get stability somehow.

1022
01:00:38,260 --> 01:00:42,640
So, somebody in Chile had the idea, all right, maybe we

